  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 232 PM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nSunday through early next week, large upper level ridge over \nthe Dakotas and Minnesota starts sliding and amplifying towards\nthe east as the low pressure system in the Pacific Northwest \nregion begins moving east into Southern Canada. This will cause \nthe pattern to shift into zonal flow over the majority of \nNorthern CONUS on Wednesday. Upper level southwesterly flow is \nstill expected until the low pressure system completely moves \nthrough transitioning to a more westerly flow. With diurnal \nheating and deepening embedded shortwave troughs, there is high \nconfidence (75%) that a weak storm could happen Sunday evening \nover the northern portions of the forecast area. While there is \nstorm supporting mixed layer (ML) CAPE value of 640 J/kg, storm \nmotion will be relatively slow. Heavy rainfall would be the main\npotential hazard as precipitable water (PWats) is forecast to \nbe 1.45 inches.\n\nThroughout the remainder of the period, chances for afternoon \nand evening showers do increase as the low pressure continues \nto move further northeast. Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) \nfor Monday evening will be 35-40% in northern Yuma county and \ntaper down to 10% towards Gove county. Tuesday afternoon looks \nto be similar to Sunday afternoon as a weak cold front will pass \nthrough from the low pressure system with ML CAPE value at \n840J/kg and a disorganized wind shear profile. There is low to \nmedium confidence (35-40%) that this will happen as there is \ninconsistency within the guidance. PoPs for Wednesday evening is\nranging from 30-35% throughout the entire area, but there is \nlow confidence that this will stay as the pattern changes. \n\nTemperatures are forecast to start above normal with the\npotential to drop back into seasonal temperatures with Sunday\nand Monday highs being in the low to mid 90s. There is growing\nuncertainty with highs on Tuesday and Wednesday due to the\npotential cold front moving through. NBM 75th to 25th\npercentiles have a 9 degree difference from the forecasted\ntemperature with potential temperatures dipping further into the\nupper 70s and low 80s.",
